# Untitled

Single-Crystal to Single-Crystal Mechanical Contraction of Metal-Organic Frameworks through Stereoselective PostSynthetic Bromination
WestChem School of Chemistry, University of Glasgow, University Avenue, Glasgow G12 8QQ, UK Supporting Information Placeholder
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs),1 porous coordination networks of metal clusters connected by organic linkers, have received wide-spread interest for use in a number of applications including, but not limited to, gas capture and sequestration,2 catalysis3 and drug delivery.4 Drawbacks associated with MOFs include their relatively low chemical and thermal stabilities,5 although recent findings suggest that ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4^{+}}$ linked MOFs,6 in particular those where $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}$ clusters link dicarboxylate struts into the UiO-66 isoreticular series,6d exhibit dramatically improved chemical7 and mechanical8 stabilities. Post-synthetic modification (PSM), where the pre-formed MOF undergoes further chemical transformation(s) whilst retaining its crystalline structure, allows incorporation of additional functionality.9 PSM of $Z\mathbf{r}.$ - MOFs has typically been achieved through ligand metalation,10 metal ion/ligand exchange10c,11 and/or direct chemical transformations on the MOF itself. Post-synthetic bromination of MOFs has been demonstrated as an attractive route for functionalization but, like the majority of covalent modifications, occurs mainly on pendant moieties.13 There are only a limited number of examples where PSM has been carried out on integral ligand sites which result in a change in length and/or geometry of the linker14,15 and, as far as we are aware, none in a single-crystal to single-crystal (SCSC) fashion to allow full structural characterisation.16  Herein, we describe the synthesis and stereoselective post-synthetic bromination of two MOFs – $\mathrm{[Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(e d b)_{6}]_{n}}$ (1) and $\left[\mathrm{Hf_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(e d b)_{6}}\right]_{\mathrm{n}}$ (2) – which are isostructural and contain $4{,}4^{\prime}{\cdot}$ -ethynylenedibenzoate $(\mathrm{ed}\mathrm{b}^{2-})$ organic linking units (Figure 1). The combined chemical and mechanical stabilities of the MOFs facilitate their quantitative, stereoselective bromination in a SCSC manner, resulting in incorporation of new functionality and permitting crystallographic characterisation of the significant mechanical contraction which the transformation induces.
The parent MOFs, $(\mathbf{\boldsymbol{\mathsf{1}}})^{\imath_{7}}$ and $(\pmb{2})$ , were synthesised by an amino-acid modulated protocol, with $4^{-5}$ equivalents of Lproline and one of HCl added to a solvothermal synthesis of $_{4},4^{\prime.}$ -ethynylenedibenzoic acid ${\bigl(}\mathrm{edb{-}H}_{2}{\bigr)}^{1\xi}$ and $\mathrm{\mathbf{MCl}}_{4}$ $\mathbf{(M=Zr}$ Hf) in $N,N^{\prime}.$ -dimethylformamide (DMF), yielding single crystals of both MOFs (Figure 1b). Typically, large equivalents of benzoic or acetic acid have been used to promote crystallisation of Zr MOFs,19 but we have found amino acids to be highly efficient modulators in low concentrations (see SI, Section S2). Incorporation of alkyne functionalities into MOFs has been highlighted as a route to achieve stimuli responsive materials,20 and both structures show large atomic displacement parameters for the carbon atoms at the centre of the edb2- ligands, suggesting the unsaturated triple bond imparts the MOFs with a degree of flexibility6e as well as potential reactivity. The well-documented stability7,8 of UiO type MOFs led us to the realisation that bromination of $\operatorname{ed}\mathbf{b}^{2-}$ to edb- $\mathrm{Br}_{2}^{2-}$ should be possible within the MOF cavity.
Brominations of both (1) and $\mathbf{\lambda}(\mathbf{{2}})$ were initially attempted (see SI, Section $S_{3}$ ) on bulk microcrystalline powder samples, with the materials suspended in $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ , exposed to bromine and left to stand in the dark for $^{48}$ hours. After work-up, the extent of chemical transformation in the brominated materials, $\left(\mathbf{1-Br}_{2}\right)$ and $\left(\mathbf{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{2}\right)$ , was analysed by a number of techniques, which are detailed below for the $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOF $\left(\mathbf{1}{-}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{2}\right)$ but also replicated for the Hf analogue $\left(\mathbf{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{\mathbf{2}}\right)$ (see SI). Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD, See SI, Section $S_{4}$ ) shows that (1) remains crystalline after post-synthetic bromination, with a small shift of the peaks of $\left(\mathbf{1}{-}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{2}\right)$ toward higher 2θ angles when compared with (1), associated with an overall decrease in size of the unit cell.
From the thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) profiles (Figure 2a), excluding initial solvent loss from the MOF pores $\cdot<$ 200 ${}^{\circ}C,$ ), it can be seen that $\mathbf{\Sigma}(\mathbf{\bar{\mathbf{\rho}}_{1}})$ exhibits typical thermal behaviour for $Z\mathbf{r}$ -MOFs, with the large mass loss around $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ attributed to combustion of the ligand.6d However, $\left(\mathbf{1}{-}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{2}\right)$
displays a two-step profile, with the initial mass loss between 350-500 $^{\circ}C$ accounting for $29.6\%$ of the mass of the desolvated material; the bromine content of fully desolvated $\left(\mathbf{1}{-}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{2}\right)$ is calculated to be $29.8\%$ . This very close correlation, alongside elemental analysis data (measured Br content $26.2\%$ ), suggests quantitative bromination of (1) has been achieved, and although this apparent de-bromination step occurs around $350~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the underlying thermal stability appears to be unaltered. $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption isotherms collected at $_{77}\mathrm{~K~}$ (Figure 2b) show a dramatic decrease in surface area, from $3280{\mathrm{~m}}^{2}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ for (1), to 2000 $\mathrm{m^{2}g^{-1}}$ for $\left(\mathbf{1}{-}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{2}\right)$ . The higher mass of $\left(\mathbf{1}{-}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{2}\right)$ cannot fully account for this decrease in gravimetric surface area and, in concert with calculated pore size distributions showing a reduction in the major pore diameter from $12.5\mathring\mathrm{\mathrm{A}}$ to 11 $\mathring\mathrm{A}$ (see SI, Section S6), suggests that the transformation from alkyne to dibromoalkene units has been successful, resulting in a mechanical contraction of the ligand and thus the MOF.
Comparison of the Raman spectra of (1) with $\left(\mathbf{1-Br}_{2}\right)$ (Figure 3a) provides striking evidence that quantitative bromination has occurred, due to the complete disappearance of the stretch at $2225\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ which is typical of alkyne units conjugated with aromatic rings. The appearance of a peak at $1640~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ in $\left(\mathbf{1-Br}_{2}\right)$ corresponds to the stretching of the newly formed dibromoalkene moiety.14
The presence of two doublets in the $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ NMR spectra (See SI, Section S8) of acid-digested samples of both (1) and (1- $\mathbf{Br}_{2})$ , whose splitting and chemical shifts were observed to change, indicates the presence of a different, single species in each. Whilst this suggests a quantitative transformation, the presence of only aromatic protons provides limited information. The presence of only 6 resonances in the $^{13}{\cal C}$ NMR spectrum of digested $\left(\mathbf{1}{-}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{2}\right)$ (Figure $^{3\mathrm{b})}$ ) is indicative that only one of the possible isomers of the dibromoalkene (cis and trans) has been obtained, i.e., the post-synthetic modification has occurred stereoselectively. Due to the geometrical constraints imposed on the edb ligand bound within (1), it is hypothesised that sole generation of the transdibromoalkene occurs, yet NMR spectroscopic characterisation is complicated by conflicting literature reports.21 The solution phase bromination of dimethyl $^{4,4^{\prime}-}$ ethynylenedibenzoate $(\mathrm{edb}\ –\mathrm{Me}_{2})$ under similar reaction conditions results in the formation of two geometrical isomers, cis- and trans-edb- $\mathbf{Me}_{2}{-}\mathbf{Br}_{2},$ as evident from both $\mathrm{^{1}H}$ and $^{13}{\cal C}$ NMR spectra (See SI, Section S9), which show resonances for both isomers. There has been some conflicting evidence in the literature concerning the $^{13}{\mathrm{C}}$ NMR chemical shifts of the resonances of dibromostilbene alkenyl carbon atoms; our results unambiguously show that the signals observed around $\delta=\mathrm{{\small{n}}}8$ ppm correspond to the trans isomer, while those around $\delta=\iota_{24}$ ppm correspond to the cis species. The geometrical constraints imposed within MOFs ensure stereoselective addition, resulting exclusively in the transbromoalkene product.22
Crystals of (1) and $\mathbf{\lambda}(\mathbf{{2}})$ were suspended in $\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ and exposed to an excess of $\mathbf{Br}_{2}$ for four days, before washing and resolvating in DMF. The brominated MOFs $\left(\mathbf{1}{-}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{2}\right)$ and (2- $\mathbf{Br_{2}},$ ) were examined by single crystal X-ray diffraction (Figure 4a) and found to retain the same $F m-3m$ space group as their parent structures, with bromination resulting in a shortening of the unit cell edges from $30.2386(_{4})\mathring\mathrm{A}$ for $\mathbf{\Sigma}(\mathbf{\bar{\mathbf{\rho}}_{1}})$ to $_{29.861\left(2\right)\mathrm{~\AA~}}$ for $\left(\mathbf{1}{-}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{2}\right)$ . This mechanical contraction of the frameworks – a $3.7\%$ decrease in unit cell volume – occurs without damaging the crystal and is a result of the edb2- linker shortening on bromination to edb- $\cdot\mathrm{Br}_{2}^{2^{-}}$ , which is evident when examining other crystallographic parameters such as solvent accessible void space (see SI, Section S10). Comparison of the calculated PXRD pattern from the crystal structure of $\left(\mathbf{1-Br}_{2}\right)$ with the experimental pattern of the bulk brominated material (Figure 4b) confirms that the stereoselective bromination proceeds quantitatively and produces phase pure material. The bent edb- $\cdot\mathrm{Br}_{2}^{2-}$ linkers lie disordered along the [110] mirror plane and are geometrically frustrated as a result of the non-linearity that originates from the $s p^{2}$ carbon atoms at their centres. The ligands adopt two different positions (Figure 4c), each of which has its own end-to-end orientational disorder (Figure 4d). Despite the disorder, it is clear that the bromination has proceeded stereoselectively to yield only trans-edb- $\cdot\mathrm{Br}_{2}^{2^{-}}$ ; similar end-to-end disorder has previously been observed in the crystal structure of trans-dibromostilbene (Figure 4e).22a The crystal structure of $\left(\mathbf{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{B}\mathbf{r}_{2}\right)$ is isostructural, with an unambiguously trans-edb- $\mathbf{Br}_{2}$ linker and similar mechanical contraction (see SI Section S10).
In conclusion, we have demonstrated a rare example of post-synthetic modification of an integral component of a MOF linker in a SCSC manner, stereoselectively and quantitatively brominating internal alkynes in the $Z\mathbf{r}-$ and Hf-MOFs (1) and (2). The process has been extensively characterised both spectroscopically and structurally, as bromination results in both the chemical and mechanical transformation of the MOFs. This simple method of changing the hybridisation of carbon atoms in MOF linkers should allow facile tuning of mechanical properties of MOFs through chemical introduction of flexibility. We are currently investigating further reactions across unsaturated bonds to introduce a variety of pore functionalities and geometries, while halogenation of alkynes may prove a useful strategy for irreversible chemisorptive capture of radioactive $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ from nuclear energy processing and/or accidents.23 Indeed, a sample of (1) exposed to $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ vapours for one week in a proof-of-concept experiment showed $61\%$ iodination of its edb2- linkers, corresponding to $41\%w/w$ irreversible trapping of $\mathrm{I}_{2}$ (see SI, Section S11).
